title: "Java Formatter: Format Java Code Online (Prettier)"
description: "Format Java code online with Prettier and prettier-plugin-java. Choose 2 or 4 space indents and line width. Runs in your browser; code is never uploaded."
permalink: /tools/java-formatter/
h1: "Java Formatter"
quickAnswer: "Paste a Java class and press Format. Prettier with prettier-plugin-java puts opening braces on the same line, indents each block by 4 spaces (or 2 for Google style), adds spaces around operators and after commas, and wraps long expressions at the line width you choose. If the code does not parse, for example because of a missing semicolon or brace, it reports the line and column instead."
workedExamples:
  - title: "A class written on one line"
    before: |-
      public class  OrderTotal{private final List<Integer> prices;public OrderTotal(List<Integer> prices){this.prices=prices;}public int total(){int sum=0;for(int p:prices){sum+=p;}return sum;}}
    after: |-
      public class OrderTotal {

          private final List<Integer> prices;

          public OrderTotal(List<Integer> prices) {
              this.prices = prices;
          }

          public int total() {
              int sum = 0;
              for (int p : prices) {
                  sum += p;
              }
              return sum;
          }
      }
    result: "Each member moves to its own line, braces open on the same line as the declaration, blocks are indented by 4 spaces, and a blank line separates the members."
  - title: "A for loop with an if inside"
    before: |-
      class Report{void print(List<String> items){for(int i=0;i<items.size();i++){if(items.get(i).isEmpty()){continue;}System.out.println(i+": "+items.get(i));}}}
    after: |-
      class Report {

          void print(List<String> items) {
              for (int i = 0; i < items.size(); i++) {
                  if (items.get(i).isEmpty()) {
                      continue;
                  }
                  System.out.println(i + ": " + items.get(i));
              }
          }
      }
    result: "The loop header gets spaces after each semicolon and around operators, and the body is expanded onto separate, indented lines."
  - title: "A stream with lambdas"
    before: |-
      class Adults{List<String> names(List<Person> people){return people.stream().filter(p->p.getAge()>=18).map(p->p.getName().toUpperCase()).sorted().collect(Collectors.toList());}}
    after: |-
      class Adults {

          List<String> names(List<Person> people) {
              return people
                  .stream()
                  .filter(p -> p.getAge() >= 18)
                  .map(p -> p.getName().toUpperCase())
                  .sorted()
                  .collect(Collectors.toList());
          }
      }
    result: "The chain is too long for one line, so each call moves to its own line, and each lambda gets spaces around the arrow and the comparison."
commonMistakes:
  - title: "Missing semicolon or brace"
    description: "Java cannot be parsed without its semicolons and matching braces, so the formatter stops and reports where the parser expected one. A missing closing brace is often reported at the end of the file, because that is where the parser runs out of code."
  - title: "Pasting a fragment instead of a class"
    description: "Prettier formats complete Java source. If you paste only a few statements, wrap them in a class and method (for example class Example { void run() { ... } }) so they can be parsed."
  - title: "Mixing 2-space and 4-space projects"
    description: "Oracle's conventions use 4 spaces and Google Java Style uses 2. Choose the indent your project already uses, or every file you format will show whitespace-only changes in review."
  - title: "Expecting imports to be sorted or removed"
    description: "The formatter keeps imports in the order you wrote them and does not remove unused ones. Use your IDE's organise-imports action or a linter for that."
  - title: "Expecting names to be fixed"
    description: "Formatting never renames classes, methods or variables. Java naming conventions, such as UpperCamelCase classes and lowerCamelCase methods, are up to you and your linter."
faqs:
  - q: "How do I format Java code online?"
    a: "Paste your Java class into the box, choose an indent and line width, and press Format or Ctrl+Enter. The formatted code appears below, ready to copy. It runs in your browser, so your code is not uploaded."
  - q: "Should Java use 2 or 4 spaces for indentation?"
    a: "Both are common. Oracle's original Java code conventions use 4 spaces, and the Google Java Style Guide uses 2 spaces with a 100-column limit. Use whatever your project already uses."
  - q: "What is the Google Java Style Guide line length?"
    a: "Google Java Style uses a column limit of 100 characters. Choose 2 spaces and a print width of 100 here to follow it."
  - q: "Where do opening braces go in Java?"
    a: "On the same line as the class, method or statement that opens the block, with the closing brace on its own line. Both the Oracle conventions and Google Java Style use this layout, and the formatter applies it."
  - q: "Why does the formatter say the code could not be parsed?"
    a: "The code is not valid Java, often because of a missing semicolon, an unbalanced brace or parenthesis, or a fragment that is not inside a class. The error shows the line and column where parsing failed."
  - q: "Does the formatter sort my imports?"
    a: "No. Imports keep the order you wrote them. Most IDEs can organise imports for you, and Google Java Style puts static imports in one group and non-static imports in another, each in ASCII order."
  - q: "Is my code uploaded to a server?"
    a: "No. Prettier, its Java plugin and the Java grammar are served from this site and run in your browser after they download the first time you format."
