    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="conventions-heading">
      <h2 id="conventions-heading">Java formatting conventions</h2>
      <p>Java has two widely used style references. Oracle's <em>Code Conventions for the Java Programming Language</em> date from 1999 and are now archived, but most Java code still follows their layout. The <em>Google Java Style Guide</em> is a newer, stricter standard used by many teams and open-source projects. Both agree on the basics this formatter applies:</p>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li><strong>Braces on the same line.</strong> The opening brace goes at the end of the line that starts the class, method, <code>if</code>, <code>for</code> or <code>try</code>; the closing brace sits on its own line, aligned with the start of that statement.</li>
        <li><strong>Consistent indentation.</strong> Oracle's conventions use 4 spaces per level and keep lines under 80 characters. Google Java Style uses 2 spaces per block and a 100-column limit.</li>
        <li><strong>One statement per line,</strong> with spaces around binary operators, after commas and after keywords such as <code>if</code> and <code>for</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Wrapped long expressions.</strong> Method chains such as streams are broken one call per line, so each step is easy to read.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Prettier's Java style is close to both, but it is its own consistent style: it chooses line breaks automatically and does not try to reproduce every detail of either guide. Use 4 spaces and 80 columns for an Oracle-like layout, or 2 spaces and 100 columns for a Google-like one.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="naming-heading">
      <h2 id="naming-heading">Naming conventions and imports</h2>
      <p>A formatter changes layout only, so naming is still up to you. The usual Java conventions are <code>UpperCamelCase</code> for classes and interfaces (<code>OrderTotal</code>), <code>lowerCamelCase</code> for methods and variables (<code>calculateTotal</code>, <code>itemCount</code>), <code>UPPER_SNAKE_CASE</code> for constants (<code>MAX_RETRIES</code>), and all-lowercase package names (<code>com.example.billing</code>).</p>
      <p>Imports are left in the order you wrote them, and unused imports are not removed. Google Java Style puts all static imports in one block and all non-static imports in another, each in ASCII order, with no wildcard imports. Most IDEs can organise imports this way for you.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="java-errors-heading">
      <h2 id="java-errors-heading">When the code will not format</h2>
      <p>Prettier has to parse your code before it can print it again. If the parser cannot make sense of it, the formatter does not guess: it reports the line and column of the first missing or unexpected token, and the cursor jumps there in the input. The usual causes are:</p>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li><strong>A missing semicolon</strong> at the end of a statement, reported as a missing <code>;</code> right after it.</li>
        <li><strong>An unbalanced brace or parenthesis.</strong> A missing closing brace is often reported at the end of the code, where the parser runs out of input.</li>
        <li><strong>A fragment outside a class.</strong> Statements need to be inside a method, and methods inside a class.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="java-privacy-heading">
      <h2 id="java-privacy-heading">Private by design</h2>
      <p>This page runs Prettier with prettier-plugin-java, which parses Java with a tree-sitter grammar compiled to WebAssembly. All of it is served from this site and downloaded only when you first format code on this page; after that it runs in your browser, so your source code never leaves your device. For Python, use the <a href="/tools/python-formatter/">Python Formatter</a>.</p>
    </section>
